From 7a41a69f285bba6be80874e6ed3ede14894b39fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zzl <zzl@google.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 10:27:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [Model] glm5_next: prefix caching on the unified KV pool

Prefix caching on a hybrid model needs the recurrent state in scheduler
blocks (mamba_cache_mode=align), i.e. the unified pool. Each pool region is
GLM-5.3's native caches instead of one attention-shaped tensor:

- sparse MLA keeps its (nope, rope) split cache, kernels unchanged;
- the DSA pool-key cache is folded into the MLA group (rides its block
  table) instead of a group of its own, which would double the footprint;
- KDA state lives in the nope rows of the block it owns (pooled Kimi op,
  one token per row via the new pool_tokens_per_row);
- the indexer member state is the first kpool nope rows of its state
  block, read and written through pool_adapters (an XLA gather/scatter on
  the uint8 pool relayouted the whole pool every call: +25 ms per decode
  step).

Block size becomes the next power of two that holds one KDA layer's state
(1088 rows -> 2048). Enabled by TPU_VLLM_ENABLE_UNIFIED_BLOCK_POOL=1 with
--enable-prefix-caching; the default (per-layer caches) is unchanged.

Also cache the sequence-parallel indexer op builder: re-registering one
custom_op name per layer could lose the op's schema under torch.compile.

Validated at 200K / TP8: hit vs miss and shared-prefix hits are
bit-identical up to 123K prefixes, GSM8K 600 94.83%, 199K-prefix TTFT
7.04 s -> 0.21 s. Cold 200K decode is still 8-13% slower than the
per-layer layout (not yet profiled).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
